{This study evaluates unsupervised learning to identify and group distinct physiological and activity profiles using multimodal data collected with the Empatica EmbracePlus.} \newtext{Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM) and Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering were compared using one-minute records and data aggregated into 10-minute windows.} \newtextblue{The 10-minute windows were formed by averaging ten consecutive, non-overlapping records.} The selection and interpretation of the clusters were based on the BIC, the Silhouette index, the elbow method, dendrograms, and Decision Trees. Temporal aggregation increased the GMM Silhouette index from 0.3475 to 0.3742 and that of the hierarchical model from 0.3959 to 0.4132. The hierarchical solution with time windows achieved the best sleep identification in the external validation (100.00\%), but had zero performance for physical activity; with the original data, the GMM achieved 75.17\% for physical activity and 82.64\% for computer work. The Decision Trees reproduced the clusters with 92.01\% accuracy in cross-validation for the original data and 83.92\% for the time windows. The results indicate that the clusters may represent patterns compatible with rest, activity, cognitive load, and physical exertion, but performance depends on the algorithm, temporal resolution, and evaluated situation. \newtext{The approach may support physiological monitoring, although larger datasets and prospective validation are required before operational use.} \newtextblue{The clusters are not direct or independently validated measures of fatigue; their relationship with perceived fatigue requires validation using independent measures.}
